1
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:22,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is an excerpt from Majjhima Nikaya 35, the Shorter Discourse on Sācaka.

2
00:00:24,645 --> 00:00:36,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, Sācaka, also known as Ajīvesanā, asked the Buddha, This is how I discipline my disciples, Ajīvesanā, and this is how my instruction is usually presented to my disciples.

3
00:00:38,574 --> 00:00:38,814
[SPEAKER_00]: bhikkhus

4
00:00:47,105 --> 00:00:48,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Material form is impermanent.

5
00:00:49,386 --> 00:00:51,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is impermanent.

6
00:00:51,788 --> 00:00:53,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception is impermanent.

7
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:55,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations are impermanent.

8
00:00:56,791 --> 00:00:58,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is impermanent.

9
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:02,579
[SPEAKER_00]: bhikkhus, material form, is not self.

10
00:01:03,139 --> 00:01:04,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is not self.

11
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception is not self.

12
00:01:07,541 --> 00:01:09,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations are not self.

13
00:01:09,882 --> 00:01:11,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is not self.

14
00:01:12,724 --> 00:01:14,644
[SPEAKER_00]: All determinations are impermanent.

15
00:01:15,525 --> 00:01:17,846
[SPEAKER_00]: All things are not self.

16
00:01:20,347 --> 00:01:26,169
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why does the Buddha describe things as

17
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:35,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Not-self rather than impermanent-like determinations.

18
00:01:35,366 --> 00:01:35,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Say it again?

19
00:01:36,226 --> 00:01:44,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why doesn't he describe the things as not-self?

20
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:47,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, as impermanent-like?

21
00:01:47,436 --> 00:01:49,218
[SPEAKER_00]: He describes determinations as impermanent.

22
00:01:49,238 --> 00:01:49,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

23
00:01:49,678 --> 00:01:53,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because that would be on the level of an explanation.

24
00:01:54,202 --> 00:01:56,304
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be on the level of everything is impermanent.

25
00:01:56,943 --> 00:01:58,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is not-self.

26
00:01:58,344 --> 00:02:04,329
[SPEAKER_02]: And then people would just use that to basically paste it over their experience and say, yeah, everything is not-self, everything is not-self.

27
00:02:04,389 --> 00:02:05,790
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point is to see for yourself.

28
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:14,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means the impermanence and the not-self are on two different levels, if you see it rightly.

29
00:02:16,218 --> 00:02:19,801
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to see the impermanence rightly, in order to experience the not-self.

30
00:02:21,362 --> 00:02:25,165
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you just think that not-self is seen where all this impermanence is seen,

31
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you'll see that with yourself, with your sense of self.

32
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:33,567
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why all determinations are impermanent, all things are not-self.

33
00:02:34,368 --> 00:02:36,910
[SPEAKER_02]: Not all determinations are impermanent, all determinations are not-self.

34
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:40,994
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, all determinations are not-self, but that's not the insight you need to see.

35
00:02:41,355 --> 00:02:48,602
[SPEAKER_02]: That, in and of itself, would just be a... just another kind of information in the view you could just adopt, which is exactly what people do today.

36
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:53,641
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, yeah, the Buddha, you know, has said everything is impermanent, and you can see everything is impermanent.

37
00:02:53,661 --> 00:02:58,782
[SPEAKER_02]: This is not myself, this is impermanent, that house is impermanent, this car, everything is impermanent, everything is not self.

38
00:02:59,422 --> 00:02:59,562
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

39
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:00,522
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is impermanent.

40
00:03:01,242 --> 00:03:02,563
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not myself.

41
00:03:03,863 --> 00:03:14,465
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's then when you also have to recognize that this impermanence of determinations, of things that determine you and define you, is not the impermanence of random things in the scientific world.

42
00:03:15,546 --> 00:03:17,626
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the impermanence of things that are dear to you,

43
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:23,233
[SPEAKER_02]: that you're bound with, that your sense of self is generated by.

44
00:03:23,253 --> 00:03:27,255
[SPEAKER_02]: So, seeing that as impermanent, means your experience won't be taken as mine.

45
00:03:29,096 --> 00:03:35,339
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to understand impermanence, you don't, like, focus on sensations in my knees?

46
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:36,040
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

47
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:40,482
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more deciding the context... Well, no, just find what's what's dear to you.

48
00:03:40,642 --> 00:03:41,983
[SPEAKER_02]: What would you be afraid of losing?

49
00:03:42,223 --> 00:03:43,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what impermanence is.

50
00:03:44,148 --> 00:03:47,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Like where I find gratification, where I seek pleasure.

51
00:03:47,609 --> 00:03:49,230
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, where my sense of self resides.

52
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:56,053
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to start undermining it by seeing it that it has to sit on something that's impermanent.

53
00:03:56,393 --> 00:03:57,553
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be defined by.

54
00:03:59,074 --> 00:04:05,696
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like when the Buddha spoke about a king, he would say he was defined by his king's possessions, by his king's army, king's wives, king's palaces.

55
00:04:05,776 --> 00:04:07,177
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what makes him a king.

56
00:04:08,687 --> 00:04:21,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have to allow that, like, yes, that sense of self to arise without acting out of it, in order to then be able to discern the determinations of it.

57
00:04:21,956 --> 00:04:22,977
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, sure.

58
00:04:23,237 --> 00:04:28,300
[SPEAKER_02]: But you really need to start... You won't even be seeing the sense of self and whether you act or not.

59
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:33,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you need to... First, you need to sort of stop looking for impermanence on the level of observation.

60
00:04:34,124 --> 00:04:38,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, whether it's tactile sensations or sights and sounds, it's impermanent, it's impermanent.

61
00:04:38,852 --> 00:04:44,714
[SPEAKER_02]: And just reflect and see what are the things that you are sort of attached to?

62
00:04:44,754 --> 00:04:46,034
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the things that are dear to you?

63
00:04:46,374 --> 00:04:48,275
[SPEAKER_02]: What were the things that you'd be afraid of losing?

64
00:04:49,635 --> 00:04:52,496
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are the determinations that are impermanent.

65
00:04:52,916 --> 00:04:54,437
[SPEAKER_02]: See, they determine you for what you are.

66
00:04:54,457 --> 00:04:57,518
[SPEAKER_02]: They determine the value of things for you.

67
00:04:57,978 --> 00:05:01,159
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what you want to see as impermanent and thus it cannot be yourself.

68
00:05:02,845 --> 00:05:05,166
[SPEAKER_02]: And initially, that's quite, like, scary.

69
00:05:05,286 --> 00:05:11,009
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for a king, to see that I'm a king because of what makes me a king, like the crown... Yeah.

70
00:05:11,089 --> 00:05:17,693
[SPEAKER_02]: All a king would need to do is just to start reflecting that he could lose all of those things, including he himself as a king.

71
00:05:17,713 --> 00:05:18,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Wouldn't be no more.

72
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:24,936
[SPEAKER_02]: And if he does not develop his mind... And if he will, he's not, he could, he will... Oh, yeah, he will, of course, of course.

73
00:05:24,956 --> 00:05:25,577
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a hundred percent.

74
00:05:25,597 --> 00:05:30,639
[SPEAKER_02]: But if somebody just starts to practice, then they'll not necessarily be able to, like, just fully recognize it's a hundred percent

75
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,041
[SPEAKER_02]: But it would still be useful to even just start thinking, yeah, I could.

76
00:05:35,462 --> 00:05:39,344
[SPEAKER_02]: See, I could already means I... okay, so I can't take this for granted.

77
00:05:39,424 --> 00:05:41,925
[SPEAKER_02]: And if it cannot be taken for granted, it cannot be myself.

78
00:05:42,545 --> 00:05:43,706
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be my ownership.

79
00:05:44,406 --> 00:05:47,348
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not the master controller if it can change against my will.

80
00:05:49,029 --> 00:05:52,831
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the king doesn't need to destroy his palaces to realize anicca.

81
00:05:52,931 --> 00:05:55,732
[SPEAKER_02]: He just needs to start questioning what he has taken for anicca.

82
00:05:56,073 --> 00:05:56,413
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

83
00:05:57,213 --> 00:05:58,314
[SPEAKER_02]: What he's taken as permanent.

84
00:05:58,334 --> 00:05:58,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

85
00:06:00,736 --> 00:06:27,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, because a lot of people think, like, when I meditate, it should be pleasurable, like, there should be, um, so, so this is actually saying, okay, there's this something quite scary that, yeah, in this contemplation, and that's good, that's something which you should initiate, yeah, not... Well, yeah, that's where the gradual training, you don't just start, you sit down and you enter these contemplations and so on, like, you have such sense restraint, withdrawal from unwholesome sensuality,

86
00:06:28,365 --> 00:06:38,814
[SPEAKER_02]: That will give you the capacity to then hold these uncomfortable things internally, without sort of, you know, looking away immediately, or trying to get rid of it, or cover it up, or distract yourself from it.

87
00:06:40,816 --> 00:06:49,564
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I keep saying, like, you know, even whatever... however you would have arrived at the practice, whatever meditation technique you're doing, really what you need to study is, like, why am I doing this?

88
00:06:49,584 --> 00:06:50,665
[SPEAKER_02]: You've got to start questioning that.

89
00:06:51,365 --> 00:06:55,229
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I... like, okay, so you're observing sensations, or...

90
00:06:56,017 --> 00:07:00,678
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, focusing on some objects and so on is like... what's the... what's the point of that?

91
00:07:01,279 --> 00:07:01,699
[SPEAKER_02]: Do I know?

92
00:07:02,099 --> 00:07:02,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you see?

93
00:07:02,919 --> 00:07:04,079
[SPEAKER_02]: What is this supposed to result in?

94
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:06,920
[SPEAKER_02]: It calms me down.

95
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:08,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Calms me down in regard to what?

96
00:07:11,502 --> 00:07:14,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Where does the disturbance come from, so that I need to practice calmness?

97
00:07:14,923 --> 00:07:16,143
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it that disturbs me?

98
00:07:16,923 --> 00:07:19,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it the fact that I was not focused enough?

99
00:07:21,105 --> 00:07:21,885
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it the fact that

100
00:07:23,482 --> 00:07:38,748
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm holding dear things that are not in my control and when I feel even the slightest hint at them perhaps changing or becoming otherwise, I'm overwhelmed with stress and pain and being unable to keep the lid on it.

101
00:07:38,768 --> 00:07:43,810
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the root of suffering, not your lack of focusing on undeveloped concentration method.

102
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:44,650
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

103
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:52,853
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the wide basis of things taken for granted as mine that cannot be yours and you're turning a blind eye on it and that's why you

104
00:07:53,178 --> 00:07:55,200
[SPEAKER_02]: The stress is building up, the pressure, the edge.

105
00:07:56,461 --> 00:07:59,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Start relinquishing it, and there'll be nothing able to perturb you.

106
00:08:01,425 --> 00:08:04,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you never ever sit again in the lotus posture to meditate.

107
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:09,012
[SPEAKER_01]: You relinquish it by seeing.

108
00:08:09,592 --> 00:08:10,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing it as Nietzsche.

109
00:08:10,153 --> 00:08:12,535
[SPEAKER_02]: You relinquish it exactly by that contemplation.

110
00:08:12,575 --> 00:08:13,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, wait a minute.

111
00:08:13,516 --> 00:08:13,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

112
00:08:14,136 --> 00:08:14,957
[SPEAKER_02]: This is dear to me.

113
00:08:16,462 --> 00:08:17,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this in my control?

114
00:08:18,086 --> 00:08:19,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it subject to being otherwise?

115
00:08:19,549 --> 00:08:20,010
[SPEAKER_02]: All these things.

116
00:08:20,430 --> 00:08:21,553
[SPEAKER_02]: And sustain those contexts.

117
00:08:22,074 --> 00:08:22,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't ignore them.

118
00:08:22,715 --> 00:08:23,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't take them for granted.

119
00:08:23,717 --> 00:08:25,982
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is relinquishing those things.

120
00:08:27,447 --> 00:08:29,248
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that...

121
00:08:30,328 --> 00:08:32,689
[SPEAKER_01]: I came across the other day the translation.

122
00:08:32,909 --> 00:08:35,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it says all saṅkharas are other.

123
00:08:36,491 --> 00:08:38,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And it wasn't alien, foreign.

124
00:08:38,432 --> 00:08:39,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't using the term anicca.

125
00:08:40,133 --> 00:08:44,514
[SPEAKER_01]: It was using other, foreign, alien, which is anicca.

126
00:08:44,694 --> 00:08:46,795
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like all saṅkharas are other.

127
00:08:47,596 --> 00:08:49,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all the things that are determining myself,

128
00:08:50,937 --> 00:08:54,981
[SPEAKER_01]: They're alien to me.

129
00:08:55,041 --> 00:09:00,265
[SPEAKER_02]: Fundamentally, they cannot be familiar, friendly, personal, dear.

130
00:09:00,406 --> 00:09:00,986
[SPEAKER_02]: They're alien.

131
00:09:01,006 --> 00:09:01,627
[SPEAKER_02]: They're unknown.

132
00:09:01,647 --> 00:09:03,208
[SPEAKER_02]: They are subject to change.

133
00:09:03,248 --> 00:09:04,289
[SPEAKER_02]: They cannot be accessed.

134
00:09:05,170 --> 00:09:07,412
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in the core, they remain alien to me.

135
00:09:08,072 --> 00:09:14,315
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why this sense of me, that depends on that alien core, well, it's equally alien to me.

136
00:09:14,576 --> 00:09:16,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think that's it.

137
00:09:17,137 --> 00:09:23,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And that which is alien to you, foreign to you, other than you, yourself, depending on that thing, how is that?

138
00:09:23,801 --> 00:09:25,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that a great... Is that dear?

139
00:09:25,221 --> 00:09:25,942
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that agreeable?

140
00:09:26,122 --> 00:09:27,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that trustworthy?

141
00:09:27,483 --> 00:09:31,105
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that something you can relax in and put your faith in?

142
00:09:31,325 --> 00:09:31,525
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

143
00:09:32,204 --> 00:09:32,364
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

144
00:09:32,484 --> 00:09:33,265
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's alien.

145
00:09:33,305 --> 00:09:34,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's unknown.

146
00:09:34,206 --> 00:09:35,147
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's uncertain.

147
00:09:35,547 --> 00:09:36,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I don't know this person.

148
00:09:36,728 --> 00:09:38,209
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know this environment.

149
00:09:38,229 --> 00:09:39,130
[SPEAKER_02]: I better be careful.

150
00:09:39,290 --> 00:09:41,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the core of your sense of self.

151
00:09:41,432 --> 00:09:45,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Unknownable, because it's completely alien and inaccessible.

152
00:09:45,196 --> 00:09:47,538
[SPEAKER_02]: So, oh, I better be careful and not take it for granted.

153
00:09:47,558 --> 00:09:49,239
[SPEAKER_01]: But I trust my body, you know.

154
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:50,541
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got good health.

155
00:09:51,850 --> 00:09:53,371
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels, you know, certain.

156
00:09:53,411 --> 00:09:55,113
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels friendly.

157
00:09:55,853 --> 00:09:56,294
[SPEAKER_02]: Familiar.

158
00:09:56,554 --> 00:09:56,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Familiar.

159
00:09:57,054 --> 00:09:57,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's it.

160
00:09:57,975 --> 00:10:01,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Familiarity due to negligence in turning a blind eye.

161
00:10:02,259 --> 00:10:03,319
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the familiarity.

162
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:12,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is becoming familiar with the context of that which is alien, which is... Yeah, become familiar with what undermines your day-to-day familiarity.

163
00:10:12,267 --> 00:10:14,809
[SPEAKER_02]: And that will not be felt pleasantly initially, at least.

164
00:10:16,670 --> 00:10:19,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you cannot... What's the idea?

165
00:10:23,717 --> 00:10:26,779
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's that other term for, yeah, anicca, isn't it?

166
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Unfamiliar, isn't it?

167
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:28,901
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, actually.

168
00:10:28,981 --> 00:10:29,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Dear.

169
00:10:29,541 --> 00:10:30,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca is dear.

170
00:10:30,642 --> 00:10:32,964
[SPEAKER_02]: Dear to you, homely, yeah, friendly, familiar.

171
00:10:34,165 --> 00:10:35,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca is the opposite of all of that.

172
00:10:37,408 --> 00:10:40,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how we take everything as familiar.

173
00:10:41,010 --> 00:10:45,794
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I'm saying, like, so it's... if you want to practice contemplation of anicca, you don't start on random things to...

174
00:10:46,971 --> 00:10:49,632
[SPEAKER_02]: That you're impersonal towards the chair, that you don't care.

175
00:10:49,652 --> 00:10:52,173
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, that bottle or that chair.

176
00:10:52,753 --> 00:10:53,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody.

177
00:10:53,453 --> 00:10:54,173
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like science.

178
00:10:54,534 --> 00:10:54,714
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

179
00:10:55,094 --> 00:11:04,497
[SPEAKER_02]: But if that chair has emotional significance, you know, it's left by your grand-grandparents and it's really valuable, oh, yeah, then contemplate the impermanence of how somebody come and steal it.

180
00:11:05,777 --> 00:11:07,618
[SPEAKER_02]: And see how you would feel on account of that.

181
00:11:08,875 --> 00:11:09,675
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, contain it.

182
00:11:09,756 --> 00:11:11,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Contain that context.

183
00:11:11,737 --> 00:11:13,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't distract yourself towards sensuality.

184
00:11:13,318 --> 00:11:14,758
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't turn a blind eye towards it.

185
00:11:15,419 --> 00:11:21,362
[SPEAKER_02]: And enduring that context removes, basically, your dependence on things that are alien.

186
00:11:22,323 --> 00:11:23,823
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's diminishing your sense of self.

187
00:11:24,024 --> 00:11:24,344
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

188
00:11:27,045 --> 00:11:29,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Do that sufficiently, there'll be nothing in the world you can take as yours.

189
00:11:32,330 --> 00:11:35,431
[SPEAKER_01]: You can get rid of things, throw things away.

190
00:11:35,491 --> 00:11:37,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can throw away.

191
00:11:37,572 --> 00:11:39,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Stuff from my grandparents.

192
00:11:39,213 --> 00:11:44,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as I said, the king can maybe destroy his castle thinking that will free him from his, but he won't.

193
00:11:45,976 --> 00:11:51,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the ownership was not in those things, it was in your attitude towards them, which means he'll take something else that's going to define him.

194
00:11:52,279 --> 00:11:56,381
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe he doesn't want to be a king anymore, but he's going to take another definition of himself.

195
00:11:56,461 --> 00:11:59,322
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, his self will find a way to get re-established elsewhere.

196
00:11:59,462 --> 00:11:59,662
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

197
00:12:02,182 --> 00:12:15,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And this insight isn't dependent on, yeah, things changing, because there's nothing to do with the things in themselves, it's your ownership of the things, which is what you're referring to, with this difference between things and determination.

198
00:12:15,825 --> 00:12:23,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

199
00:12:23,567 --> 00:12:30,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, eh... Sachika, or Ajivasana, then refutes the Buddha.

200
00:12:36,882 --> 00:12:47,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as when seeds and plants, whatever their kind, reach growth, increase and mature, all do so in dependence upon the earth, based upon the earth.

201
00:12:48,612 --> 00:13:07,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And just as when strenuous works, whatever their kind, are all done in dependence upon the earth, based upon the earth, so too, Master Gautama, a person has material form as self, and based upon material form, he makes good and bad choices.

202
00:13:07,944 --> 00:13:12,747
[SPEAKER_00]: A person has feeling as self, and based upon feeling, he makes good and bad choices.

203
00:13:14,068 --> 00:13:15,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he goes through five aggregates.

204
00:13:18,688 --> 00:13:20,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Ajivasana.

205
00:13:22,231 --> 00:13:23,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you not asserting thus?

206
00:13:24,372 --> 00:13:25,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Material form is myself.

207
00:13:26,753 --> 00:13:27,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is myself.

208
00:13:27,954 --> 00:13:29,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception is myself.

209
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:31,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations are myself.

210
00:13:31,536 --> 00:13:32,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is myself.

211
00:13:34,498 --> 00:13:36,239
[SPEAKER_00]: And he then asks a counter question.

212
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:39,441
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think, Ajivasana?

213
00:13:40,860 --> 00:13:53,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Would a head-anointed noble king exercise the power in his own realm to execute those who should be executed, to find those who should be fined, and to banish those who should be banished?

214
00:13:55,105 --> 00:13:59,567
[SPEAKER_00]: He would exercise it, Master Gautama, and he would be worthy to exercise it.

215
00:14:01,728 --> 00:14:05,809
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think, Ajivasana, when you say thus?

216
00:14:05,929 --> 00:14:07,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Material form is myself.

217
00:14:08,883 --> 00:14:21,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you exercise any such power over that material form, as to say, let my form be thus, let my form not be thus, and then he goes through each of the five aggregates.

218
00:14:25,356 --> 00:14:37,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person can understand this intellectually, like, I don't have power, ultimate power over, like, form or feeling or

219
00:14:39,411 --> 00:14:47,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, any of these aggregates... How does this insight, yeah, free one from suffering?

220
00:14:47,897 --> 00:15:01,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, yeah... Well, by exactly what I said before, you have to do it on the level of things that are taken as yours, on the level of things that you do assume the ownership over.

221
00:15:02,067 --> 00:15:03,588
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, a king

222
00:15:05,125 --> 00:15:12,286
[SPEAKER_02]: You can see whether he's in charge or not by trying to exercise power within his kingdom, not on someone else's kingdom.

223
00:15:12,926 --> 00:15:15,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, they wouldn't tell him anything.

224
00:15:15,447 --> 00:15:16,527
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what people do.

225
00:15:16,587 --> 00:15:17,007
[SPEAKER_02]: They try.

226
00:15:17,307 --> 00:15:22,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, formula, but all those rhapsody things, they don't actually stand for what the form is, for what the feeling is.

227
00:15:22,488 --> 00:15:24,109
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why they're not uprooting anything.

228
00:15:24,989 --> 00:15:30,970
[SPEAKER_02]: But, obviously, even Sachika understood that he was referring to the actual form.

229
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:31,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

230
00:15:32,094 --> 00:15:33,655
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that he was just this kind of...

231
00:15:33,675 --> 00:15:34,115
[SPEAKER_02]: This self.

232
00:15:34,295 --> 00:15:35,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's why.

233
00:15:35,096 --> 00:15:39,898
[SPEAKER_02]: He wasn't just like a kind of some abstract theoretical kind of just trying to argue with the Buddha.

234
00:15:40,419 --> 00:15:46,722
[SPEAKER_02]: He was referring to the form, which it is there, which it is the basis, you know, we've been taking the self and so on.

235
00:15:47,143 --> 00:15:56,228
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just that he thought, see, that form is always there, that feeling is always there, so whether you act like this or you act like that, that's all secondary to the fact that you are it.

236
00:15:56,468 --> 00:15:56,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

237
00:15:57,649 --> 00:16:01,391
[SPEAKER_02]: But then the Buddha said, okay, that form that you do see rightly, let's say like that

238
00:16:02,250 --> 00:16:03,951
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, what are the properties of it?

239
00:16:04,311 --> 00:16:06,172
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you make it not deform?

240
00:16:06,372 --> 00:16:07,452
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you make it not age?

241
00:16:07,472 --> 00:16:09,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you make it not experience elements?

242
00:16:09,833 --> 00:16:11,194
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he realizes, oh, he can't.

243
00:16:11,794 --> 00:16:13,955
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, you're not a king.

244
00:16:15,076 --> 00:16:18,597
[SPEAKER_02]: If you... if a king cannot exercise his power, he's not a king.

245
00:16:19,178 --> 00:16:20,178
[SPEAKER_02]: Somebody else is.

246
00:16:21,159 --> 00:16:22,059
[SPEAKER_02]: Or it's not his kingdom.

247
00:16:23,563 --> 00:16:39,475
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you want to reflect that with practical results, as I said, it needs to be done on the level of your kingdom, on the level of things that are dear to you, that you assume the ownership, that you feel entitled towards and justified in.

248
00:16:39,595 --> 00:16:46,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, oh, now start reflecting upon the form over there, upon the feeling over there, upon the perceptions over there.

249
00:16:48,558 --> 00:17:02,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing the five aggregates in the way that Sachaka was doing, is kind of like a necessary prerequisite for then looking at the determinations of that self.

250
00:17:02,822 --> 00:17:05,163
[SPEAKER_02]: I think the problem is that people, as you said, due to like

251
00:17:06,516 --> 00:17:14,065
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, it's been so long since dhamma was kind of alive, in the sense Buddha was teaching it, people don't even know what the aggregates are.

252
00:17:14,586 --> 00:17:23,597
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you read about the aggregates, you get these abstract notions, and then you reflect on the level of abstract notions, and then you proliferate further in the views, and that's exactly like...

253
00:17:24,338 --> 00:17:31,264
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know how many times I heard, yeah, modern science has shown that, proven the Buddha right, because look, everything is constantly changing.

254
00:17:31,704 --> 00:17:41,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, yes, people observing sensations and trying to pinpoint anicca and see and let go of, yes, all on these kind of proliferated abstract terms and views, while in reality you're still

255
00:17:41,852 --> 00:17:58,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Bound and attached with your sensual desires, love of children, family, company, safety, health, taking life for granted, all these vast bases that you could really look close, not even that closely, and you would realize, oh, this is unownable.

256
00:17:59,118 --> 00:18:08,100
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's completely ignored and then just been kind of reinterpreted and re-explained through these views and, yeah, electrons changing or this or sensations coming and going.

257
00:18:08,661 --> 00:18:09,481
[SPEAKER_02]: All that's relevant.

258
00:18:10,201 --> 00:18:11,722
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not where your ownership is.

259
00:18:11,742 --> 00:18:12,862
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not where your sense-self is.

260
00:18:12,882 --> 00:18:14,803
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all, like, on the secondary terms.

261
00:18:15,583 --> 00:18:17,864
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not even where the five aggregates are, so to speak.

262
00:18:17,884 --> 00:18:18,665
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you're not...

263
00:18:18,685 --> 00:18:22,586
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, it's all within the five aggregates, but you're not seeing them correctly.

264
00:18:23,206 --> 00:18:23,987
[SPEAKER_02]: As a context.

265
00:18:24,387 --> 00:18:24,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

266
00:18:24,707 --> 00:18:25,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that which undermines you.

267
00:18:27,868 --> 00:18:36,652
[SPEAKER_02]: And Sāchika does... he sees that as that which undermines whether you do this or that, feel this or that, but then he assumes that which undermines is yours.

268
00:18:37,392 --> 00:18:37,813
[SPEAKER_02]: It's there.

269
00:18:38,053 --> 00:18:38,653
[SPEAKER_02]: It's stable.

270
00:18:40,830 --> 00:18:43,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's changing all the time, at least it is still mine.

271
00:18:43,791 --> 00:18:44,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

272
00:18:44,392 --> 00:18:45,572
[SPEAKER_02]: He wasn't obviously seeing it.

273
00:18:45,892 --> 00:18:47,593
[SPEAKER_02]: He wasn't seeing the change on the right level.

274
00:18:48,314 --> 00:18:51,255
[SPEAKER_02]: He was seeing the change as secondary to the form, to the feeling.

275
00:18:52,115 --> 00:18:55,797
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why he was regarding it as permanent, as mine, as myself.

276
00:18:57,018 --> 00:18:57,858
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's what we're pointing out.

277
00:18:57,878 --> 00:18:58,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, okay.

278
00:18:59,039 --> 00:19:04,941
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, that form which you refer to, can you prevent it from deforming, being subject to illness?

279
00:19:05,341 --> 00:19:06,242
[SPEAKER_02]: The feelings, can you

280
00:19:06,963 --> 00:19:09,928
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you make them go away when you want and bring only the ones you want?

281
00:19:10,068 --> 00:19:10,229
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

282
00:19:10,349 --> 00:19:12,552
[SPEAKER_02]: You are subjected to them in the same way.

283
00:19:12,613 --> 00:19:13,254
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just like, oh...

284
00:19:14,682 --> 00:19:15,903
[SPEAKER_02]: None of that can be mine.

285
00:19:16,643 --> 00:19:20,264
[SPEAKER_02]: But you need sense-restraint and virtue to be able to see that.

286
00:19:20,584 --> 00:19:22,085
[SPEAKER_00]: To reveal what is dear to you.

287
00:19:22,105 --> 00:19:24,006
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll think, okay, I can control my feelings.

288
00:19:24,046 --> 00:19:27,107
[SPEAKER_00]: I can eat a tasty cake and that will feel better.

289
00:19:27,287 --> 00:19:27,467
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

290
00:19:27,527 --> 00:19:35,110
[SPEAKER_02]: The virtue and sense-restraint would force you to stop turning a blind eye to what is dear to you and actually feel the weight of it.

291
00:19:35,270 --> 00:19:38,251
[SPEAKER_02]: And you know you're subjected to it because, oh yeah, it's unpleasant.

292
00:19:39,272 --> 00:19:42,433
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognizing that these things are subject to change while you're bound with them,

293
00:19:45,373 --> 00:19:45,954
[SPEAKER_02]: It's unpleasant.

294
00:19:46,034 --> 00:19:46,935
[SPEAKER_02]: It's what anxiety is.

295
00:19:47,095 --> 00:19:48,356
[SPEAKER_02]: It's what suffering is.

296
00:19:48,917 --> 00:19:51,859
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't have been unpleasant.

297
00:19:51,879 --> 00:19:51,999
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

298
00:19:52,059 --> 00:19:52,500
[SPEAKER_02]: If it was.

299
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:55,002
[SPEAKER_02]: If you were not bound with them.

300
00:19:55,022 --> 00:19:56,864
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were in control of it.

301
00:19:57,004 --> 00:19:58,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if you were in control of them.

302
00:19:58,305 --> 00:19:59,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

303
00:19:59,846 --> 00:20:05,832
[SPEAKER_02]: That's that sutta where we said, monks, would you be upset if people come and start collecting these branches and twigs and burning them and stuff?

304
00:20:05,872 --> 00:20:06,492
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

305
00:20:06,532 --> 00:20:08,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, we're not emotionally bound with them.

306
00:20:08,874 --> 00:20:09,675
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't care about them.

307
00:20:10,562 --> 00:20:17,967
[SPEAKER_02]: It says, well, so should you practice towards your own body, towards your own feelings, towards your own aggregates, regarding the same as these random things in the world, these heaps.

308
00:20:19,488 --> 00:20:29,754
[SPEAKER_02]: But, in order to do that, you have to undo all those kind of bonds, so to speak, and assumptions, and things you hold dear to you, that you take for granted.

309
00:20:30,575 --> 00:20:38,620
[SPEAKER_02]: So, stop restraining, stop acting out and maintaining that assumed ownership through your actions, and then feel the weight of it, and sustain the context.

310
00:20:39,790 --> 00:20:40,311
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's it.

311
00:20:40,351 --> 00:20:41,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Mind will have to turn away from it.

312
00:20:42,393 --> 00:20:47,940
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're providing the perception of anicca in things that the mind is bound with.

313
00:20:48,020 --> 00:20:49,502
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it forces the mind to turn away.

314
00:20:49,622 --> 00:20:50,984
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, risk, risk, risk, risk.

315
00:20:51,064 --> 00:20:51,845
[SPEAKER_02]: And it goes away.

316
00:20:52,618 --> 00:20:55,919
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you turn your mind from assumptions and ownership.

317
00:20:56,419 --> 00:21:07,841
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't just decide it, but if you clarify, sustain and cultivate that context day in, day out, the mind will have to turn away, because it becomes unsuitable basis for ownership.

318
00:21:09,022 --> 00:21:12,142
[SPEAKER_02]: The suitable basis for ownership was turning a blind eye.

319
00:21:13,142 --> 00:21:13,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignorance.

320
00:21:13,683 --> 00:21:14,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignoring it.

321
00:21:14,543 --> 00:21:14,903
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

322
00:21:14,923 --> 00:21:15,583
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it took.

323
00:21:16,333 --> 00:21:22,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that's... there's assumptions where, like, how do you know that form has your best interest at heart?

324
00:21:23,654 --> 00:21:24,535
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, why do you assume that?

325
00:21:24,795 --> 00:21:26,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's that alien aspect of it.

326
00:21:26,275 --> 00:21:29,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the most... the Mother Earth, you know.

327
00:21:29,235 --> 00:21:29,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

328
00:21:29,855 --> 00:21:32,136
[SPEAKER_01]: You just assume that it's friendly, that it's for me.

329
00:21:32,656 --> 00:21:32,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

330
00:21:32,896 --> 00:21:34,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I can lift my arm, I can do...

331
00:21:35,076 --> 00:21:35,416
[SPEAKER_00]: I can...

332
00:21:35,637 --> 00:21:37,037
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm in control of my form, yeah.

333
00:21:37,137 --> 00:21:39,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a gift from Mother Earth, isn't it?

334
00:21:40,717 --> 00:21:43,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Does she even know... does she even know that you exist?

335
00:21:43,958 --> 00:21:44,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah?

336
00:21:44,760 --> 00:21:47,805
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's just a selective, selective sort of...

337
00:21:49,568 --> 00:21:56,138
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like that assumption where you assume that the God is looking after you, the Mother Earth is looking after you.

338
00:21:56,779 --> 00:21:57,160
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all...

339
00:21:58,783 --> 00:22:00,684
[SPEAKER_01]: For you, with interest.

340
00:22:00,764 --> 00:22:02,104
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all fundamentally good.

341
00:22:02,124 --> 00:22:03,804
[SPEAKER_01]: But why do you jump to that conclusion?

342
00:22:04,184 --> 00:22:05,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know.

343
00:22:05,645 --> 00:22:09,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you, at, on some level, you know that it is other.

344
00:22:10,246 --> 00:22:11,806
[SPEAKER_01]: That you've got no access to that thing.

345
00:22:12,426 --> 00:22:14,547
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got no control of it.

346
00:22:14,567 --> 00:22:16,387
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can feel the weight already, I think.

347
00:22:17,768 --> 00:22:19,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people would feel the weight of it.

348
00:22:20,048 --> 00:22:22,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Of being not in control, unless

349
00:22:23,850 --> 00:22:25,391
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's all going to work out, isn't it?

350
00:22:25,471 --> 00:22:25,671
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

351
00:22:26,391 --> 00:22:35,054
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the weight of non-control is felt, and then it's too unpleasant to, like, endure it, sustain the context in regard to it.

352
00:22:35,594 --> 00:22:45,097
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's easier to just then slip back into the view, oh, but this is all friendly and has my best interest in heart, and everything will work out.

353
00:22:45,337 --> 00:22:48,778
[SPEAKER_02]: We're all heading to heaven, because, you know, fundamentally we're all good, and so on.

354
00:22:48,798 --> 00:22:50,579
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's like, no, there's just a coping mechanism.

355
00:22:51,059 --> 00:22:53,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, maybe it's good.

356
00:22:53,900 --> 00:22:57,045
[SPEAKER_02]: See, the fact is, the emphasis on maybe, which means you can never know.

357
00:22:57,566 --> 00:22:58,547
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is it worth the risk?

358
00:23:00,550 --> 00:23:03,615
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when you say, say, yeah, it's good, it worked out well.

359
00:23:03,795 --> 00:23:06,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, but how do you know that that has worked, that that will work out well?

360
00:23:06,899 --> 00:23:07,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Next.

361
00:23:08,897 --> 00:23:15,722
[SPEAKER_02]: Because fundamentally, things you are heading to, no matter how good everything else is, is sickness, aging and death.

362
00:23:16,262 --> 00:23:18,263
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is that good?

363
00:23:18,784 --> 00:23:20,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that going to be felt lightly, pleasantly?

364
00:23:21,626 --> 00:23:24,588
[SPEAKER_02]: And by the way, it doesn't mean like just all sickness, aging and death, okay, fine, I'll deal with it.

365
00:23:25,288 --> 00:23:32,413
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, when you're sick and dying, means everything that is dear to you in the world, on account of your senses, your children, your family,

366
00:23:34,024 --> 00:23:42,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Your reputation, your name, your wealth, everything else that you acquired, oh, that's... that's now being taken away.

367
00:23:43,871 --> 00:23:53,777
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you think you can handle death, well, just check beforehand, even while you're alive and healthy, can you handle losing everything?

368
00:23:54,698 --> 00:23:56,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Right here and now, just in mundane sense.

369
00:23:57,480 --> 00:24:01,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Not from a death point of view, just if somebody were to come and take everything from you,

370
00:24:02,954 --> 00:24:06,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you be unmoved by any of that?

371
00:24:06,735 --> 00:24:09,796
[SPEAKER_00]: By the simile of the saw, if they come and chop off your legs.

372
00:24:09,836 --> 00:24:11,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, but you don't even have to go that far.

373
00:24:11,497 --> 00:24:17,219
[SPEAKER_02]: Just take away your car, take away your house, take away your family, take away stuff you're familiar with.

374
00:24:18,059 --> 00:24:19,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Would your mind be unmoved?

375
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:24,842
[SPEAKER_02]: If you think, oh no, well then, you can only imagine what death will do.

376
00:24:26,242 --> 00:24:27,223
[SPEAKER_02]: How much it will move you.

377
00:24:27,763 --> 00:24:29,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Because those are the things that will be taken away.

378
00:24:29,783 --> 00:24:31,124
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to feel exactly like that.

